### What is the owning side vs inverse side?

The owning side is the entity that has the FK column in its database table. In a one-to-many relationship between Account and Trade, the `trades` table has the `account_id` FK column. Therefore Trade is the owning side. JPA reads only the owning side for SQL generation.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// CHILD (Trade): OWNING SIDE – has the FK column
@Entity
@Table(name = "trades")
public class Trade {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "trade_id")
    private String tradeId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)  // Many trades belong to ONE account
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id", nullable = false)  // FK column
    private Account account;

    private String symbol;

    @Column(precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal amount;

    // getters, setters
}
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// PARENT (Account): INVERSE SIDE – no FK column
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
    // mappedBy = "account" → Trade.account field owns this relationship
    // cascade = ALL → persist/merge/remove propagate to trades
    // orphanRemoval = true → removing trade from list deletes it

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    private String holderName;

    @Column(precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // getters, setters
}
```

### Why are helper methods critical?

Helper methods synchronize both sides of the bidirectional relationship. Without them, the FK column can be NULL because JPA only reads the owning side.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();

    private String holderName;
    private BigDecimal balance;

    // HELPER: synchronize BOTH sides
    public void addTrade(Trade trade) {
        trades.add(trade);
        trade.setAccount(this);  // set the owning side!
    }

    public void removeTrade(Trade trade) {
        trades.remove(trade);
        trade.setAccount(null);  // clear the owning side!
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TradeCreationService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TradeCreationService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void addTradeToAccount(String accountId) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();

        Trade trade = new Trade();
        trade.setTradeId("TRD-099");
        trade.setSymbol("RELIANCE");
        trade.setAmount(new BigDecimal("245050.00"));

        // CORRECT: use helper that sets BOTH sides
        account.addTrade(trade);
        // trade.setAccount(account) called inside helper
        // INSERT INTO trades (..., account_id) VALUES (..., 'SAV2001')
    }
}
```

### What happens if you only set one side?

Setting only the inverse side (parent's list) without setting the owning side (child's FK) results in a NULL FK or a constraint violation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TradeMistakeService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TradeMistakeService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void wrongApproach(String accountId) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        Trade trade = new Trade();
        trade.setTradeId("TRD-100");
        trade.setSymbol("HDFC");
        trade.setAmount(new BigDecimal("150000.00"));

        // WRONG: only set the inverse side
        account.getTrades().add(trade);
        // trade.getAccount() is still null!
        // JPA reads the owning side (Trade.account) for SQL generation
        // INSERT INTO trades (..., account_id) VALUES (..., NULL)
        // Constraint violation: account_id cannot be NULL!
    }

    @Transactional
    public void correctApproach(String accountId) {
        Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
        Trade trade = new Trade();
        trade.setTradeId("TRD-100");
        trade.setSymbol("HDFC");
        trade.setAmount(new BigDecimal("150000.00"));

        // CORRECT: use helper that sets both sides
        account.addTrade(trade);
        // INSERT INTO trades (..., account_id) VALUES (..., 'SAV2001')
    }
}
```

### What does @JoinColumn configure?

`@JoinColumn` specifies the FK column details on the owning side.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
@Table(name = "trades")
public class Trade {
    @Id
    private String tradeId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(
        name = "account_id",          // FK column name in trades table
        nullable = false,              // NOT NULL constraint
        foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_trade_account")  // custom FK name
    )
    private Account account;

    private String symbol;
    private java.math.BigDecimal amount;
}
```

### What is unidirectional vs bidirectional?

Three patterns exist for one-to-many relationships. Bidirectional with helper methods is the recommended approach.

```java
// Pattern 1: UNIDIRECTIONAL @ManyToOne (Trade → Account)
// Trade has @ManyToOne Account account
// Account does NOT have @OneToMany List<Trade> trades
// Use when: you never need account.getTrades()

// Pattern 2: BIDIRECTIONAL (Trade ↔ Account) – RECOMMENDED
// Trade has @ManyToOne Account account (owning)
// Account has @OneToMany(mappedBy) List<Trade> trades (inverse)
// Use when: you need both account.getTrades() AND trade.getAccount()

// Pattern 3: UNIDIRECTIONAL @OneToMany (Account → trades)
// Account has @OneToMany List<Trade> trades (WITHOUT @ManyToOne on Trade)
// AVOID: JPA creates a join table (accounts_trades) instead of a FK!
// Less efficient, harder to understand
```

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// AVOID: unidirectional @OneToMany without @ManyToOne
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class AccountBad {
    @Id
    private String accountId;

    @OneToMany  // NO mappedBy, NO @ManyToOne on Trade
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
    // JPA creates: accounts_trades join table!
    // INSERT INTO accounts_trades (account_id, trade_id) VALUES (?, ?)
    // Less efficient than a simple FK column
}
```

### What are cascade and orphanRemoval?

`cascade` propagates operations from parent to children. `orphanRemoval` deletes children that are removed from the parent's collection.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id
    private String accountId;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account",
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL,    // persist/merge/remove propagate
               orphanRemoval = true)          // remove from list = DELETE from DB
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();

    private String holderName;
    private java.math.BigDecimal balance;

    public void addTrade(Trade trade) {
        trades.add(trade);
        trade.setAccount(this);
    }

    public void removeTrade(Trade trade) {
        trades.remove(trade);
        trade.setAccount(null);
        // orphanRemoval: DELETE FROM trades WHERE trade_id = ?
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Trade;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class CascadeDemo {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public CascadeDemo(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void cascadePersist() {
        Account account = new Account();
        account.setAccountId("SAV2001");
        account.setHolderName("Rajesh Kumar");
        account.setBalance(new BigDecimal("500000.00"));

        Trade t1 = new Trade("TRD-001", "RELIANCE", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        Trade t2 = new Trade("TRD-002", "HDFC", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        account.addTrade(t1);
        account.addTrade(t2);

        accountRepo.save(account);
        // CascadeType.ALL propagates persist:
        // INSERT INTO accounts VALUES ('SAV2001', 'Rajesh Kumar', 500000)
        // INSERT INTO trades VALUES ('TRD-001', 'RELIANCE', 100000, 'SAV2001')
        // INSERT INTO trades VALUES ('TRD-002', 'HDFC', 200000, 'SAV2001')
        // ONE save() call persists parent + all children!
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Senior engineers should understand these nuances about entity relationships:

- **Fetch strategy interaction.** `@ManyToOne(fetch = LAZY)` prevents N+1 at the entity level, but JPQL `SELECT t FROM Trade t` still fires N+1 if you access `t.getAccount()` in a loop. Use `JOIN FETCH` or `@EntityGraph` to load relationships in a single query.
- **Batch size for collections.** `@BatchSize(size = 50)` on `@OneToMany` loads children in batches of 50 instead of one query per parent. This is a Hibernate-specific optimization that mitigates N+1 without changing the query.
- **equals and hashCode.** In bidirectional relationships, avoid using the collection side in `equals()`/`hashCode()`. Use the business key or the database ID to prevent infinite recursion and Hibernate proxy issues.
- **Ordering collections.** Use `@OrderBy("amount DESC")` on `@OneToMany` for database-level ordering or `@OrderColumn` for a persistent index column. Do not rely on insertion order.
- **Bidirectional toString danger.** If both Account and Trade include each other in `toString()`, you get a `StackOverflowError`. Exclude the parent from the child's `toString()` or use `@ToString.Exclude` with Lombok.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.Objects;

@Entity
@Table(name = "trades")
public class Trade {
    @Id
    private String tradeId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    private Account account;

    // Use business key for equals/hashCode, NOT the collection
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Trade t)) return false;
        return tradeId != null && tradeId.equals(t.tradeId);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hashCode(tradeId);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with relationships?

The three most common mistakes are: not setting both sides, forgetting `fetch = FetchType.LAZY` on `@ManyToOne`, and using unidirectional `@OneToMany` without `@ManyToOne`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "trades")
public class Trade {
    @Id
    private String tradeId;

    // WRONG: default EAGER on @ManyToOne
    // @ManyToOne
    // @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    // private Account account;
    // 200 trades = 200 Account SELECTs (N+1)

    // CORRECT: explicit LAZY
    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id", nullable = false)
    private Account account;

    private String symbol;
    private BigDecimal amount;
}
```
